+# Reliable Failed/Aborted Stream Accounting Implementation Plan
+
+> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.
+
+**Goal:** Make Gatekeep write a `RequestLog` row (with estimated tokens/cost, an `outcome`, and accurate latency) for every streamed request that fails mid-stream or is aborted by a disconnecting client, and for non-streaming provider errors, instead of silently losing accounting for those requests.
+
+**Architecture:** Move the three accounting calls (`StreamTimer.finish`, `log_request`, `observe_request`) out of the `StreamEnd`-only branch of both SSE generators (`_sse`, `_messages_sse` in `gatekeep/app.py`) and into a `finally` block that runs on every exit path, tagging each row with a new `outcome` column (`ok` / `provider_error` / `client_disconnect`). On a mid-stream failure there is no authoritative token count, so tokens are estimated from accumulated delta text via a new `estimate_tokens` heuristic (the same ~4-chars/token approximation `embeddings.py` already uses). `StreamTimer.finish` gains a `succeeded` flag so a failed stream's `duration_ms` is anchored to the last real token, not the failure moment. The non-streaming provider-error path gets the same `outcome`-tagged row (zero tokens, no estimation possible). Dashboard latency queries exclude failed rows from percentiles via the new `outcome` column; a new success-rate stat surfaces them instead. A generator's `finally` block runs during `GeneratorExit`/`CancelledError`, and a disconnecting client can inject cancellation more than once while the DB commit is in flight, so the accounting write is wrapped in a small cancellation-shielding helper that guarantees it completes before the exception propagates.
+
+**Tech Stack:** Python 3.11+, FastAPI/Starlette, SQLAlchemy async + asyncpg, Alembic, pytest + pytest-asyncio + httpx (ASGITransport), Prometheus client, React + TypeScript (dashboard frontend), Vite.
+
+## Global Constraints
+
+- Never use the em dash "—"; use a plain dash "-" instead, including in code comments and docstrings.
+- Docstrings are required on every new/modified function, method, and class (Google-style, matching this codebase's existing docstring style: purpose, Args, Returns, Raises where applicable).
+- No backwards-compatibility shims, no feature flags, no defensive error handling for scenarios that can't happen - trust internal invariants the way the existing code does.
+- `finally` blocks in the SSE generators must never `yield` (raises `RuntimeError` during `GeneratorExit`) - accounting only (DB writes, metric observations).
+- Every new/changed piece of behavior needs a failing test written first (TDD), matching this codebase's existing test style (real Postgres/Redis via `tests/conftest.py`, no mocking of the DB).
+- Run `ruff check gatekeep tests` and `ruff format --check gatekeep tests` before considering any task with Python changes complete; fix any findings ruff reports in code this plan touches.
+- The `outcome` column values are exactly `"ok"`, `"provider_error"`, `"client_disconnect"` (matching the design spec verbatim) - no other strings.
+- `estimate_tokens` rounds up (`ceil`) so any non-empty text counts as at least one token; empty text is zero tokens. This mirrors `embeddings.py`'s existing "~4 characters per token" heuristic (`_MAX_CHARS = 1000 * 4`), which rounds the other direction for a length *limit* - the two are independent, do not unify them.
